<!--Post: 5295--><!--Time: 1107036865-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST5295"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Lilymarie<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Lilymarie<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, January 29, 2005 - 2:14 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->BTK has a typical problem that many who grew up with Spanish as a first language have &#40;as well as other native languages&#41; when writing in English. That is using &#34;correct verb tense&#34; and tenses in general. &#34;I&#39;m sorry this happen to society&#34;, &#34;Where this monster enter my brain&#34;, &#34;victim being torture and being mine&#34;, &#34;it here to stay&#34;. Those are just a few of several examples in the letters. If one went to school in the USA, with English as a first language, it would be a very peculiar style of writing as an adult.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 5295-->
<!--Post: 5297--><!--Time: 1107038445-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST5297"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Angrywolf<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Angrywolf<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, January 29, 2005 - 2:40 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->That&#39;s a good observation...If BTK is Hispanic one would think that would have narrowed the field, although with the package out in the county it&#39;s possible that BTK does not live in Wichita proper..How many Hispanic men of his probable age live within 100 miles of Wichita for example ?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 5297-->
<!--Post: 5300--><!--Time: 1107040176-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST5300"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Lilymarie<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Lilymarie<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, January 29, 2005 - 3:09 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Thanks, Angrywolf. You&#39;re right - if one believes BTK has a Hispanic or Latin background - then it would be logical to take the typical profile a bit further. Most Hispanic immigrants are Catholic. Many who immigrated to Wichita worked in the railways and large manufacturing industries that were the heart of the city. The Otero&#39;s &#40;or at least Joseph&#41; were from Puerto Rico. Hopefully others have more ideas?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 5300-->
<!--Post: 5331--><!--Time: 1107054738-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST5331"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Pax56<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Pax56<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, January 29, 2005 - 7:12 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Hi folks.  I agree there is a language thing going on.  I don&#39;t know if BTK is faking the language problems in the early letters, as some people think, but even if he is, he chose as his model the exact problem you note.  Because I am married to a non-native English speaker, I can fake her speech patterns very effectively.  I actually mimic them when I need to make sure she understands me the first time &#40;like when a semi-truck is about to run us over&#41;.  But in any case, my point is that BTK either had the pattern you note, or was closely exposed to it &#40;like our kids&#41;.  In that case, he could have an Hispanic or Romance-European parent &#40;Spain, Italy Portugal, etc.&#41;.   <BR> <BR>Even the children of one ethnic parent often have speech disorders, reflective of the ethnic parent&#39;s usage and vocalization, which can lead to a host of problems if not treated &#40;speech therapy was not as pervasive in schools back then&#41;.  We have multiple cases of this in my family tree.  It can lead to underestimation of a person&#39;s intelligence, as I believe was the case for BTK. <BR> <BR>I do agree that BTK is bi- or multilingual, and at least one of those languages is probably Spanish, Portuguese, or Italian.  However, I now believe that PetraTina was really Pet raT in a [cage], as some others have speculated, and not a real Hispanic acquaintance &#40;I could be wrong - time will tell&#41;. <BR> <BR>Mixed heritage, possibly Hispanic, could also account for some of the scatter in eyewitness accounts of BTK.  Ethnicity is often in the eye of the beholder. <BR> <BR>Your idea would also help to explain BTK&#39;s fascination with simple word games.  I am endlessly fascinated by simple patterns and whatnot in my wife&#39;s language - things which she never noticed as a native speaker.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 5331-->
<!--Post: 5336--><!--Time: 1107056899-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST5336"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Stealth<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Stealth<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, January 29, 2005 - 7:48 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->He could have also been mimicking the Oteros or just trying to make it seem like someone you describe...is this based off the first letter or their examples in his recent stuff?  <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 5336-->
<!--Post: 5337--><!--Time: 1107057107-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST5337"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Lilymarie<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Lilymarie<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, January 29, 2005 - 7:51 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Thanks for the real-life insight, Pax. I live in Texas and have many hispanic friends. Most of my closer Latina friends have gone through school in the US their whole lives, but many of their parents have not. I don&#39;t notice this &#34;tense&#34; issue when we visit - but I do sometimes notice it in written form. On the Petra Tina aspect - I think the girl is named Petrina. A very common name in Puerto Rico. I do, however, admire those that are willing to put the effort into the word and numerology puzzles. My attention span is much too short to tackle that stuff.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 5337-->
<!--Post: 5341--><!--Time: 1107058262-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST5341"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->George<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->George<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, January 29, 2005 - 8:11 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I also think English may be BTK&#39;s second language. I would, however, like to see more examples of the writing, just to be sure. IMO, it really wouldn&#39;t have been that difficult for BTK to fake the phraseology so he came off as Hispanic or Hispanic related. I don&#39;t know, it seems <i>too</i> obvious. Does the phrasing of the &#34;Oh, Anna&#34; poem contain the same tendencies as the letters? Just wondering.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 5341-->
<!--Post: 5342--><!--Time: 1107058434-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST5342"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Pax56<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Pax56<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, January 29, 2005 - 8:13 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Hi Stealth - I think most opinions are directed to the first letter - especially now that we have &#40;nearly&#41; the full text.  At least, that is what I&#39;m basing my opinion on.  But you are right - I never considered that it could be a part of his misdirection.  If so, he is doing an exceptional job, in my opinion.  But then SKs are probably very good at that sort of thing. <BR> <BR>Something I did look for, and am not sure if I found, was something to indicate that he was faking.  Several people noted things like use of slang and American idioms that indicated a good familiarity with English, but this would be consistent with all scenarios - a fast-learning non-native, a child of a non-native, and a faker.  What are your thoughts?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 5342-->
<!--Post: 5344--><!--Time: 1107059398-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST5344"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Lilymarie<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Lilymarie<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, January 29, 2005 - 8:29 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->George - I hadn&#39;t thought of comparing the horrendous &#34;Oh, Anna&#34; fiasco with the letters - but I did just now. I only see one possible tense problem in his poem. Now, we could look at that two different ways: 1. He actually wrote &#34;Oh, Anna&#34; as an original work &#40;uhggg&#41; and he&#39;s faking his tense problem or, 2. he copied it from another work that he knew from his childhood &#40;in Puerto Rico, Purtugal?&#41; or from his US high school/WSU literature classes. If it was copied, more than likely he would have copied the correct tenses too. Good thought on your part.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 5344-->
<!--Post: 5352--><!--Time: 1107061513-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST5352"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->George<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->George<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, January 29, 2005 - 9:05 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Lilymarie, thanks for checking. I hope a majority may agree English as BTK&#39;s second language; as you and others say, it would narrow the suspect list/potential suspect list hugely. Thanks.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 5352-->
<!--Post: 5353--><!--Time: 1107061538-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST5353"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Johno<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Johno<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, January 29, 2005 - 9:05 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Go back to the Nancy Fox phone call folks before you get too far on this topic. <BR>It was made by a white male born in the USA. <BR>The caller was BTK. <BR>If the caller was not BTK then it was from another killer who went to Nancy Fox&#39;s apartment to kill her only to find someone else had already killed her so as his civic duty he reported the crime but did so anonymously. <BR>BTK is a white male. <BR>BTK can&#39;t even compose a letter all the way through faking just bad grammer. He alternates between correct verbage and syntax with an obvious attempt to puposely make it look like the letter is from someone uneducated or just learning the English language. He can&#39;t even write a short letter without convincingly making it seem he is somewhat illiterate with the English language.  He further shows his command of English familiarity by using idioms used only by Americans. <BR>He either is purposly trying to make obvious mistakes to hep disquise himself or he may simply be someone who when he wants to communicate writes out his thoughts in a rapid fashion and some words get mispelled and the sentences fragmented but he doen&#39;t bother to correct them or start an new letter. He leaves as is. <BR>BTK is a white male.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 5353-->
<!--Post: 5354--><!--Time: 1107062058-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST5354"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Lilymarie<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Lilymarie<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, January 29, 2005 - 9:14 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Well, Johno...thanks for your &#34;proclamations&#34;. I disagree fundamentally and you make my points without even knowing it. BTK&#39;s very short, rehearsed phone call to the PSAP is obviously an attempt on his part to &#34;sound&#34; midwestern and &#34;white&#34; &#40;which BTW includes Latina, Hispanic, Mexican Americans, etc.&#41;. Much easier to rehearse a few short statements than to write several letters and poems utilizing correct English grammar. Most of us who grew up in the good old USA can&#39;t do it consistently. I respect your opinion...even if it doesn&#39;t leave any room for you to &#34;listen&#34; to other, more thorough insights.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 5354-->
<!--Post: 5356--><!--Time: 1107063840-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST5356"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->George<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->George<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, January 29, 2005 - 9:44 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Johno, I&#39;m of the belief he&#39;s white and your forceful points I agree with almost entirely but people can speculate. We aren&#39;t police detectives here. You&#39;re one of the best poster&#39;s on the board <BR>and because of this, haven&#39;t you noticed that when people try to be original they tend to receive derision? I&#39;m guilty for doing it once, too. I&#39;ve had to thicken my hide to withstand the garbage I take and it&#39;s making me meaner by the minute. All I&#39;m saying is those with theories should be encouraged. One of both John Douglas&#39;s and Roy Hazelwood&#39;s observations about anyone wanting to be a profiler is &#34;a creative, open mind.&#34; How ironic that when an amateur on a board has an idea they are often ridiculed <i>because</i> they were creative and open-minded - mandatory traits according to Douglas and Hazelwood.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 5356-->
<!--Post: 5357--><!--Time: 1107064375-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST5357"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Lilymarie<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Lilymarie<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, January 29, 2005 - 9:52 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->This &#34;amateur&#34; will leave it to you &#34;professionals&#34;. There are certainly enough clues out there for all of us to research on our own. I didn&#39;t realize that we amateurs &#34;speculate&#34; while you of a higher mind have the ability to take the same facts decide that they are &#34;forceful points&#34;. Please, spare all me. So arrogant and really quite narrow minded.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 5357-->
<!--Post: 5358--><!--Time: 1107064631-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST5358"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Johno<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Johno<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, January 29, 2005 - 9:57 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->They are proclomation Lillymarie but they are my proclomations posted under the topic thread &#39;BTK Theories&#39; so I would assume that you would understand that they are my proclomations in context to theory.  <BR>I would have to be BTK, personally know him or have access to his DNA makeup to conclusively proclaim that BTK is a white male but under the context of &#39;BTK Theories&#39; let me again say BTK is a white male. <BR>Let&#39;s take another phone call. <BR>Don Granger, a TIPS Hotline operator at the Wichita Eagle spoke with the caller in October of 1974 who directed him to have investigators go to the Wichita Public Library to find a letter written by BTK hidden in a mechanical engineering textbook. Grange described the called as hard and agressive with no percivable accent who sounded like he was midwestern USA.  <BR>The caller was BTK. <BR>BTK is a white male. <BR>Look back at his letters and poems. He makes perfect English and alternates with bad English. He can&#39;t know both if he only knows bad English. He is either writing faster than he thinks and doen&#39;t care what mistakes he make gramatically or he is trying to disguise his literacy by tossing in some bad grammer. <BR>I&#39;ve &#39;listened&#39; to your insights but as in the reasons I&#39;ve explained I certainly don&#39;t find it any more thorough than mine.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 5358-->
<!--Post: 5359--><!--Time: 1107065133-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST5359"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->George<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->George<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, January 29, 2005 - 10:05 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Lilymarie, I was saying that people should be able to theorize without fear. I didn&#39;t attack you, I was expressing an opinion. If I believe BTK was white, so what? That can change. If I knew <BR>with certainty I&#39;d say so. Be careful with that word &#34;arrogant.&#34; <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 5359-->
<!--Post: 5360--><!--Time: 1107065227-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST5360"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Lilymarie<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Lilymarie<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, January 29, 2005 - 10:07 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Hey, I&#39;m into peace not this type of stuff. I know all of the same facts you do, the phone calls, the statements by Don Granger, the letters, poetry, etc. You&#39;re not giving me any new info that will enlighten me and change my &#34;opinion&#34;. My opinion is intended to open up minds that are set in stone and not coming up with anything new or creative. This isn&#39;t one of those ridiculous &#34;chimera&#34; dna ideas floating around here - that&#39;s where your &#34;lets get back to reality&#34; posts should be placed. Context and theory and basic analysis are only going to form a foundation - they won&#39;t find the twisted demon that drives these crimes. But good luck to ya!<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 5360-->
<!--Post: 5361--><!--Time: 1107065238-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST5361"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->George<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->George<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, January 29, 2005 - 10:07 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->&#34;Amateur&#34; was a reference to myself, Lilymarie. A trifle self-absorbed are we?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 5361-->
<!--Post: 5362--><!--Time: 1107065549-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST5362"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->George<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->George<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, January 29, 2005 - 10:12 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Oh, I see. Johno and I think BTK was white, which conflicts with your opinion, hence, we are &#34;arrogant&#34; because we don&#39;t share your belief. <BR>Get over yourself.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 5362-->
<!--Post: 5363--><!--Time: 1107065623-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST5363"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Lilymarie<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Lilymarie<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, January 29, 2005 - 10:13 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->George - sorry - I realize your intentions were in the right place. And, hey....I respect whatever gender ideas you have about BTK&#39;s ethnicity or whatever else. But that is just my point.....You are not &#34;proclaiming&#34; that it has been proven that BTK is white - and that we are wasting our time to consider alternatives. You are careful to note that that is what you think. This board wouldn&#39;t be very interesting if everyone believed that BTK is white, 65 years old, retired telephone technician, Tom V devotee, chat board lurker with the ability to change his DNA. &#40;That one kills me&#41;.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 5363-->
<!--Post: 5371--><!--Time: 1107067255-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST5371"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Lilymarie<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Lilymarie<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, January 29, 2005 - 10:40 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Ouch. Nevermind. I&#39;m over myself and this discussion too. I&#39;d still like to hear from others about English Second Language &#40;ESL&#41; theories and BTK. Thanks!<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 5371-->
<!--Post: 5376--><!--Time: 1107076532-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST5376"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Pax56<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Pax56<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, January 30, 2005 - 1:15 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->...glad I was off doing a painful video card reconfig, because it looks like the above discussion was more painful.  Lets face it folks, we&#39;re all amateurs.  The few pros on the site are generally the first ones to question themselves and to admit they might be wrong.  Mike K. for example.  I&#39;ve seen that most people here have some areas of special knowledge from their day jobs and life histories that bring added insight to various corners of the case, and that&#39;s the stuff I love to hear.  It is by friendly discussion and contrasting and/or combination of the results, with an occasional breakthrough, that we gain knowledge and progress here.  I enjoy hearing from each of you, and hope to hear more of your ideas.  Even the chimera stuff, which I find fascinating &#40;but definitely out there!&#41; <BR> <BR>Back to the ethnicity question &#40;ESL&#41; - there are indeed numerous visuals on BTK that say &#34;white male&#34;, but there is also the &#34;suspicious Hispanic-looking guy&#34; near the Otero home, and the &#34;dark-complected&#34; guy described by the Vian kids &#40;I believe that was the one - please correct me if it was a different crime&#41;.  I am bothered by both of those.  It is tempting to throw those out as extraneous for the reasons that people have done so &#40;possibly not connected, and &#34;kid&#34; witnesses, among others&#41;.  As others have mentioned, eyewitness testimony is unreliable anyway - but that cuts both ways.  The advantage of looking at some blend is obvious.  I don&#39;t see it as an either-or situation.  I think there are good reasons to take a white male and add a touch of salsa to get BTK.  But not too much.  We&#39;ve seen BTK&#39;s penchant for misdirection - we are all sensing that.  I think he would love nothing more than for us to go off chasing Antonio Banderas, when we&#39;re more likely looking at Geraldo Rivera &#40;think of their voices&#41;. <BR> <BR>Because of my job &#40;geek&#41;, I know a lot of foreign-born Americans who arrived here as small children and immediately began learning English.  They all have distinctly American accents, and vocabularies that include slang, regionalisms, and idioms.  Way different from their parents.  But they all have little peculiarities that jump out at you when you hear them a while.  That&#39;s one thing about the BTK phone call - it&#39;s a white guy to me, but it&#39;s just a bit weird.  Among the many theories people have proposed to explain the weirdness, a very light touch of ethnicity gives us a &#34;two-fer&#34; or even a &#34;three-fer&#34; on other case facts, so I like it.  I suspect that this is why some &#34;pros&#34; &#40;WPD, various profilers&#41; keep bringing up the Hispanic thing. <BR> <BR>I think I could sum it up by saying that I think English may have been his &#34;second&#34; language in a chronological sense, but I think it&#39;s really his main one - the one he grew up with at school, and probably for the most part at home and with any siblings.  He was not left with much of an accent other than Midwestern.  He may have a parent who still barely speaks English, although I would imagine that &#40;s&#41;he spoke fairly well but with a strong accent.  And probably with some bad tenses!<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 5376-->
<!--Post: 5385--><!--Time: 1107099622-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST5385"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Stealth<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Stealth<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, January 30, 2005 - 7:40 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I always just looked at the first letter as someone trying to appear crazed, choppy and cryptic.  But he has to be fairly well educated; he used words like psychotic and perversion and spelled them correctly. <BR> <BR>For example, if we gave George the task of writing a letter like BTK&#39;s first one, to create a serial killer persona and take credit for the Otero&#39;s, if George writes it in his normal style, we would be able to peg him right off.  However, George is a clever guy.  He would do something to try to throw us totally off and create an image that strikes fear without ID&#39;ing him.    <BR> <BR>The later BTK stuff is much more clear, he mostly drops the choppy style.  And he is still using his BTK SK persona.  I made 4.0 in college in the language arts requirements and I still leave out words, can&#39;t spell, and make typos. <BR> <BR>Pax - It was Kevin Bright, not Vian.  They evidently described the first police officer on the scene.  I have often wondered if the Otero description was affected by them seeing the car and them assuming it was new neighbor Joe, who was Puerto Rican and that they had seen around but they had not met yet.  They didn&#39;t think it was anything unusual at the time.  Then Kevin describes a person matching the Otero sketch.  ALL witness descriptions have been discounted by LE. <BR> <BR>Still, doesn&#39;t mean BTK is non-white.  I have just always seen &#40;and heard&#41; him that way.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 5385-->
<!--Post: 5387--><!--Time: 1107100686-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST5387"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Audreyamw<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Audreyamw<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, January 30, 2005 - 7:58 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->BTK&#39;s ethnic background could be mixed. Perhaps an Anglo father and Hispanic mother.  If LE are leaning toward a Hispanic suspect, and BTK actually had an Anglo last name, it might make him harder to peg as a suspect.  If his mother was Hispanic and he spent a decent amount of time with her and her relatives, and if they lived in a predominantly Hispanic area when he was growing up, he would have learned Spanish as either his first language, or at least at the same time that he would have been learning English. <BR> <BR>THis raises some interesting profile possibilities. With a mixed background that included a Hispanic mother/Anglo father, he would&#39;ve been exposed to quite a contrast in cultures in the home.  It&#39;s been speculated in the &#34;profile&#34; forum that BTK had a submissive mother, probably religious, and a father who was domineering and misogynist. In the &#34;posts from BTK&#34; forum, there&#39;s a post allegedly from BTK under the user name &#34;Christ on the Cross.&#34;  The reference to &#34;felling&#34; in one of his poems, reminds me of the &#34;Fall of Man&#34; in the garden of Eden, popular Catholic symbolism.  Also Lucifer as a fallen angel.  I think BTK has a derisive attitude towards Catholicism, based on this possible family/cultural situation.  If his parents relationship fits the profile given on this Web site, the mother may have been a practicing Catholic, while his father was non-religious &#40;or not Catholic anyway&#41;. <BR> <BR>Just theorizing.  The bilingual angle is interesting. I&#39;m in Canada and grew up in an area with a lot of French speakers as well as English speakers. It wasn&#39;t unusual to see families where the parents came from different language backgrounds and the children grew up speaking both languages from the beginning.  I know people from just such a background who are highly literate in both languages as well, but funny grammar errors will show up in their written language, although you won&#39;t hear a trace of an accent when they speak &#40;in either French or English&#41;.   <BR> <BR>When it comes to learning a second language, it&#39;s generally accepted that learning how to read a second language is relatively easy -- it&#39;s the skill you&#39;re least likely to lose, even if you haven&#39;t been exposed to that language in a while.  You never forget what you learn to read.  Speaking a second language is not as easy as learning to read a second language, but learning to write a second language is hardest of all.  Learning perfect written grammar is tough in one language, never mind two.  <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 5387-->
<!--Post: 5389--><!--Time: 1107101301-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST5389"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Audreyamw<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Audreyamw<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, January 30, 2005 - 8:08 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Hmm, a couple of other thoughts just occurred to me.  My own background is ethnically French - French last name, I &#34;look&#34; French -- but I grew up speaking English as my first language.  English at home, English neighbourhood, English at school.  I ended up learning French as a second language.  Part of this is due to history, when my parents generation was growing up, they were expected to assimilate and learn English.  Speaking French was seen as a sign of ignorance, because of the prejudices at the time.  A French-speaker was assumed to be backward, illiterate and basically a hick.  So the pressure was there to conform and learn English and hide the French background as much as possible. <BR> <BR>I&#39;m going to assume that in years past, the situation was very much the same for Hispanics in the Kansas area and thereabouts.  IF BTK came from a mixed ethnic background, Hispanic mother/Anglo father, with domineering father and submissive mother, this would&#39;ve given his father one more excuse to put down his mother.  Society would also have been sending this message in subtle and not-so-subtle ways.  BTK would have absorbed this. <BR> <BR>Is it possible that BTK has a condescending attitude towards Hispanics?  To the point of perhaps targeting them to some extent? Could this have been the &#34;revenge&#34; element in the Otero attack?  Could he have given LE misleading information to steer them toward the Hispanic suspect last year &#40;did BTK set up the poor guy&#41;? <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 5389-->
<!--Post: 5401--><!--Time: 1107107902-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST5401"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Lilymarie<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Lilymarie<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, January 30, 2005 - 9:58 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->All very interesting Pax, Stealth &amp; Audrey. Thanks for exploring the ESL angle with me. The bi-lingual and bi-racial aspect I hadn&#39;t considered. One more thought I had after thinking of your posts is that where I live - there are many bi-racial couples - White husband, Asian mother. The kids sometimes have an exotic look that may be confused as somewhat Hispanic. <BR> <BR>Also, Audrey - I don&#39;t know this for sure but I think you are right when you say that one can learn to read a 2nd language, speak it convincingly, but writing is the most elusive skill. My son is teaching ESL as he gets ready to head out to the Ukraine for the Peace Corps and he believes this to be the most frustrating skill for his students to learn.  <BR> <BR>Keeping an open mind will help prevent the misdirection on ethnicity and other traits that happened with the serial sharp shooters...<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 5401-->
<!--Post: 5417--><!--Time: 1107113262-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST5417"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Stealth<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Stealth<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, January 30, 2005 - 11:27 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->See, I made a darn typo...doh!...I said &#34;Still, doesn&#39;t mean BTK is non-white. I have just always seen &#40;and heard&#41; him that way.&#34;   <BR> <BR>I meant I think he&#39;s white...I have always thought he was a midwestern WASP...just cause I think that way, certainly doesn&#39;t mean he is...<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 5417-->
<!--Post: 5418--><!--Time: 1107113626-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST5418"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Stealth<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Stealth<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, January 30, 2005 - 11:33 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->&#34;Is it possible that BTK has a condescending attitude towards Hispanics? To the point of perhaps targeting them to some extent? Could this have been the &#34;revenge&#34; element in the Otero attack?&#34; <BR> <BR>I have read other posters that lived there at the time say there weren&#39;t many people of other races besides black &amp; white in Wichita during the mid 70&#39;s.  It makes me queasy, but I really think he may have been intentionally or unintentionally mimicking the Otero&#39;s. <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 5418-->
<!--Post: 5420--><!--Time: 1107115177-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST5420"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->George<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->George<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, January 30, 2005 - 11:59 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text--><i>For example, if we gave George the task of writing a letter like BTK&#39;s first one, to create a serial killer persona and take credit for the Otero&#39;s, if George writes it in his normal style, we would be able to peg him right off. However, George is a clever guy. He would do something to try to throw us totally off and create an image that strikes fear without ID&#39;ing him.</i>  <BR> <BR>I&#39;ve said the same thing all along, Stealth; BTK a WASP trying to feign a Hispanic lineage, but I would always be open-minded about new ideas; if you can, carefully re-read my post of Saturday, January 29, 2005 - 9:44 pm. <BR>If you were given the task of, say, having an original thought, well you&#39;d be caught because that&#39;s beyond your capacity. Keep teeing &#39;em up, Stealth. <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 5420-->
<!--Post: 5421--><!--Time: 1107115877-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST5421"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Lilymarie<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Lilymarie<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, January 30, 2005 - 12:11 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Geo - I think Stealth was complimenting you.It would be more productive to change the tone of this thread. Let&#39;s just agree that there are differences of opinion and style between the strong personalities here and move on.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 5421-->
<!--Post: 5422--><!--Time: 1107115896-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST5422"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->George<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->George<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, January 30, 2005 - 12:11 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->And Stealth, I&#39;ll thank you to keep your sick speculation about my psyche to yourself. Don&#39;t you dare use my name in connection with writing the Otero letter. You need a psychiatrist.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
