<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST6110"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Essevi<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Essevi<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, February 04, 2005 - 8:44 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Whoisbtk, thank you! This analysis is super! <BR> <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 6110-->
<!--Post: 6111--><!--Time: 1107535787-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST6111"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Itty<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Itty<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, February 04, 2005 - 8:49 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Thank you Whois, I have been trying to do the same thing, but an english teacher I am not. I noticed how he freq. leaves of the &#34;ed&#34;, and I thought maybe his name is ed. Putting all of these letters together that are left out may spell something, like  &#34;ed is doing it&#34; <BR>Can your teacher friend correct the other letters that have been made public?  What gets me is that the cops cant be experts at everything, like punctuation. I hope they did have an &#34;english&#34; expert look at everything. If they didnt, then I hope they read your post and say, &#34;what a good idea, lets try that&#34;. I have thought many times, if btk is so smart, or thinks he&#39;s so smart, and wants everyone to know he is so much smarter than le, why all the errors? I think he makes the mistakes on purpose, and we have to look at what is missing.  <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 6111-->
<!--Post: 6117--><!--Time: 1107537943-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST6117"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Lubbock<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Lubbock<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, February 04, 2005 - 9:25 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I still believe that BTK may be learning disabled, maybe dyslexic. This is not to say that he isn&#39;t intelligent, street smart, and cunning, just that expressing himself in written form is not his forte. In the era when he grew up, if he is/was indeed LD he would more than likely be labeled as a &#34;slow learner&#34;, which is a nice euphemism for stupid. Alot of kids wouldn&#39;t be so nice. Maybe this is why he is so ate up with proving he&#39;s smarter than everyone else. <BR> <BR>With all due respect, Lillymarie, the voice on the Fox call sounds about as caucasion as can be. Could it be faked without the slightest hint of a residual hispanic twang - I don&#39;t know, I suppose so since actors adopt accents, dialects, and even languages with enough skill to fool my ears. After seeing Lethal Weapon I was surprised to learn that Mel Gibson is Australian. That is a matter of an accent, however, and not as complex a task as mimicking the proper syntax, grammer, and inflections of a different language. I doubt that Ah-nuld could pull it off. Similarly, on occasion I work with Mexican immigrants - I think it would&#39;ve been easier to teach a parrot to make the call.  <BR> <BR>Also, someone posted that they believed the call was longer than what we hear at the portal to this site. This might add a degree of difficulty to a person rehearsing a canned statement as he would have to anticipate questions. <BR> <BR>Just my opinion, I&#39;m keeping an open mind.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 6117-->
<!--Post: 6164--><!--Time: 1107559443-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST6164"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Audreyamw<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Audreyamw<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, February 04, 2005 - 3:24 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Really fascinating, your friend did some great work. <BR> <BR>If BTK was making all those mistakes in his letters intentionally, it reveals something else about him.    Why would he pretend to be semi-literate in his letters, and then turn around and send those elaborate poems, which would seem to imply that he&#39;d be a very capable writer?  That&#39;s an interesting inconsistency.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 6164-->
<!--Post: 6171--><!--Time: 1107564398-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST6171"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Lilymarie<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Lilymarie<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, February 04, 2005 - 4:46 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Wow Who! Very nice. It is one thing to go on instinct - which is what I did. I totally respect the analysis of the examples your friend provided. And what is even more exciting is that I understand it!  <BR> <BR>Now, it makes me wonder...as others here have already made this leap....why would this guy want to &#34;appear&#34; to be a non-native? <BR> <BR>Excellent post. I respect your efforts very much.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 6171-->
<!--Post: 6186--><!--Time: 1107569225-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST6186"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Whoisbtk<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Whoisbtk<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, February 04, 2005 - 6:07 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Lilymarie <BR> <BR>That&#39;s a good question.  Why?   <BR> <BR>Hmmmm.... <BR> <BR> <BR><a href="mailto:whoisbtk@yahoo.com">whoisbtk@yahoo.com</a> <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 6186-->
<!--Post: 6221--><!--Time: 1107593699-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST6221"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Pax56<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Pax56<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, February 05, 2005 - 12:54 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Very interesting analysis - great work, you folks.  Perhaps BTK was knowledgeable of the mix of the Oteros&#39; friends and acquaintances, and if they were largely Hispanic, he may have felt like he &#34;stood out&#34; as a non-Hispanic.  He may have been scared that he would be identified too easily, unless he made his ethnicity ambiguous.  In that sense, he may have sent in his letter, not for &#34;jealousy&#34; reasons as people have supposed, but with full intent to deceive.  Maybe the cops were getting close - interviewing some non-Hispanic acquaintances of the Oteros who were leading in his direction.  I don&#39;t know, just a theory.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 6221-->
<!--Post: 6491--><!--Time: 1107824526-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST6491"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:blingbling1169@yahoo.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Cawichitan<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Cawichitan<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, February 07, 2005 - 5:02 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I can&#39;t beleive that all of the intelligent people in here would fall for the whole &#34;different language&#34; thing. It is obvious that he is intelligent and his sentence structure was better than most college educated people I have come across. I agree with the teacher from the USD259. As an employee of a college in Southern Cal, I worked in the Assessment Center for Math, English and ELS. The ELS students who have yet to master our language have also yet to master where commas and other punctuation marks go, most of us don&#39;t even know that! I can&#39;t beleive some people didn&#39;t catch that! He is simply trying to throw us off.  <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 6491-->
<!--Post: 6493--><!--Time: 1107826706-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST6493"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Skov<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Skov<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, February 07, 2005 - 5:38 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I agree Cawichitan <BR> <BR>I think his &#34;misspellings&#34; ect is done  <BR>deliberately to blur his normal writing style, possible because he have published, maybe still are publishing, som kind of writing and probably has a distinctive style.  <BR>And I belive that is over averaged talented in the field of writing. <BR> <BR>skov<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 6493-->
<!--Post: 6497--><!--Time: 1107828308-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST6497"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Pax56<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Pax56<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, February 07, 2005 - 6:05 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->You&#39;ll have to forgive those of us who have not had such contact with ESL students, Cawichitan!  I agree, the teacher&#39;s observations make the case for me.  But still, most of us have not had a lot of contact with people faking language problems.  Just another psychopathic deception trait that fools the majority, I guess.  I have worked with a wide variety of highly intelligent foreigners, and their language problems are all seemingly unique - and not nearly as clear to me as they would be to a professional who works with such people on those very language problems.  The only linguistic problems I would note would be in their code &#40;computer languages&#41;.  Reading their English in emails, I would not be able to sort a faker out of the pack.  So please bear with us - we&#39;re just brainstorming outloud, I guess! <BR> <BR>Skov - I think you&#39;re right.  There must have been something out in public, or something less public that he felt a few people could link to him.  He may have published either prose or an academic writing.  Perhaps even beat prose.  Or thought that he could be recognized by a Wichita teacher who was intimately familiar with his writing.  Again - what if he was a student of Mr. Streiff - perhaps one who he would spot by the tone of his writing?  I wish we had better access to writings of that time on the internet.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 6497-->
<!--Post: 6498--><!--Time: 1107829391-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST6498"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Lilymarie<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Lilymarie<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, February 07, 2005 - 6:23 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Hey, not all of us work in the education profession - with contact with ELS &#40;as you said&#41; ESL students on a regular basis. I&#39;m sure there are discussions here, cawichitan, where you are not in your element or an expert - and you will find some of us are. However, we will make an attempt not to use sarcasm and condescending language to help you understand our point. In fact, you will notice that most of us had already &#34;come around&#34; long before your post.  <BR> <BR>What was your point again?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 6498-->
<!--Post: 6598--><!--Time: 1107904713-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST6598"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Audreyamw<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Audreyamw<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, February 08, 2005 - 3:18 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->DNA! <BR> <BR>Just a thought on BTK&#39;s ethnic/language background.  I&#39;ve read that it&#39;s possible to determine someone&#39;s ethnicity from a DNA sample.  The can even break down someone&#39;s heritage into quantities like 25% Native American, 50% Asian, 25% Caucasian and so on.  Since the police apparently have BTK&#39;s semen to provide them with DNA, surely they would&#39;ve used the sample to determine BTK&#39;s ethnicity?  The WPD seems to be leaning towards a hispanic suspect, or at least someone with Hispanic DNA in them -- they didn&#39;t hesitate to arrest/swab the Hispanic man back in December.  BTK could look Caucasian, and talk Caucasian, but he must have Hispanic ancestry too.  I&#39;m trying to cling to the tattered remains of my Anglo father/Hispanic mother theory. <BR> <BR>And I think BTK has some weird grudge against Hispanics.  That could be his one and only reason for trying to sound non-Anglo, to point WPD towards Hispanic suspects, when he knows he&#39;d be  safe because of an Anglo name, Anglo accent, and his ability to &#34;pass&#34; as Caucasian.  <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 6598-->
<!--Post: 6609--><!--Time: 1107907502-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST6609"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:blingbling1169@yahoo.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Cawichitan<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Cawichitan<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, February 08, 2005 - 4:05 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Wow Lilly! I am not into the conflicts over the net! You and the others may feel the need to engage in that stuff, but I don&#39;t. Its funny that this is what YOU chose to take from my post. I am not negetive and don&#39;t appreciate being approached in a negetive manner. I really think you blew that way out of porportion, but I see from prior posts that you tend to do that, please miss me with the attitude. I am here to help catch a killer that has been plagued my home town for years! Is that what you are here for? Either way, it doesn&#39;t take an expert to figure out that a person who uses near perfect punctuation has to be used to or at least very familiar with the English language. I am not an expert and I could have very well figured that out without having worked in an Assessment Center! I am not one to attack others or their opinions so take my comments just like Skov and PAX 56 did, they&#39;re not angry! And how can you speak on what other will and won&#39;t do? I was not being sacastic I was just baffled at the fact that more people didn&#39;t catch that one, give me a break Lilly-geez!<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 6609-->
<!--Post: 6613--><!--Time: 1107908546-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST6613"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Lilymarie<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Lilymarie<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, February 08, 2005 - 4:22 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->CAW - you&#39;re not into conflicts over the net? You are not one to attack others or their opinions? <BR> <BR>YOUR POST: <BR> <BR>&#34;I can&#39;t beleive that all of the intelligent people in here would fall for the whole &#34;different language&#34; thing.&#34; <BR> <BR>It was my idea...I pulled some folks in to explore it...and have now changed my mind based in their great input.  <BR> <BR>ENOUGH SAID. Let&#39;s move on, okay?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 6613-->
<!--Post: 6655--><!--Time: 1107927853-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST6655"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Kcatty<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Kcatty<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, February 08, 2005 - 9:44 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Hi everybody - I&#39;m new to the board and am fascinated by everyone&#39;s analysis of who BTK is &#40;or isn&#39;t&#41;.  Here&#39;s my two cents on the language issue:  I think his misspellings are simply typos.  Notice how the letters S, E, and D are often missing:   <BR> <BR>Dude[s] = Dude <BR>Tie[d] = tie <BR>Cover[ed] = cover <BR>Hand[s] = hand <BR>stripe[d] = stripe <BR>turn[ed] = turn <BR>it[&#39;s] = it <BR> <BR>My hunch is to look for someone who has an injured left ring finger, since that&#39;s the finger used to type those letters.   &#40;I also think he&#39;s a non-descript looking white guy, somebody who has an &#34;honest&#34; face and who wouldn&#39;t look threatening if he came to your door - kinda like the guys in the Maytag Repairman commercials - but I guess that is for another topic.&#41;<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 6655-->
<!--Post: 6732--><!--Time: 1107989095-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST6732"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:blingbling1169@yahoo.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Cawichitan<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Cawichitan<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, February 09, 2005 - 2:44 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I moved on after my last post and it seems everyone else has too! I don&#39;t need you to reiterate my post to me, I wrote it! As I said before that was how YOU chose to take it-no one else did-why are YOU so offended? We are trying to catch a killer TOGETHER! I am not the enemy-Enough said?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 6732-->
<!--Post: 6745--><!--Time: 1107993599-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST6745"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:blingbling1169@yahoo.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Cawichitan<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Cawichitan<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, February 09, 2005 - 3:59 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Disclaimer- Opinion Only!!!! <BR> <BR>Back to the language thing, I want you guys to really take this into consideration. I DON&#39;T claim to be an expert, but when you live in Los Angeles as long as I have, you have a tendency to be able to distinguish one accent from another. We have several different Africans, Latinos and Asians here. I have never had such a diverse group of friends. In Wichita when I was going to school the number of Latinos increased but never by that much. I went to Riverside Elementary, John Marshall Middle School and North High School. All of these schools are on the North/North West side of town, which is the portion of town where most ALL of the hispanics lived and we still had more blacks and whites at our schools than anything! This was in the early to late 90&#39;s and I can only imagine what the population of hispanics were back in the day! I SERIOUSLY doubt after listening to that tape over and over that he was able to disguise his voice well enough to sound for a lack of a better word &#34;country&#34; when people are speculating that he may be hispanic. A hispanic person has a tendency to pronounciate things differently and they can&#39;t change that even if they wanted to&#40;it would take some serious time, effort and practice!&#41; The same thing for us &#34;country folk&#34;. We tend to over pronounciate our R&#39;s, like &#34;over thur&#34; and &#34;it&#39;s right herr.&#34; We, after many years can learn NOT to do this, but it takes years of being out of the social situation in which the others speak this way, but for most, as soon as they return to their place of orgin, the accent comes back. After moving to Los Angeles people still tease me to this day about saying &#34;hurr&#34; and not &#34;hair&#34;!  <BR> <BR>I was born and raised in Wichita and when you have an accent you just do. The person who called the operator was a white male from the US and has been here for some time, there is no doubt in my mind and to come to find that most experts feel the same just makes my theory concrete TO ME. Most killers DO NOT call 911 to report murders they committ, but BTK did and he was not worried about being caught just by voice alone, he knew it would take more than that to be caught because there are a lot of white males in Wichita-it was like &#34;well, looking for me at this point would be like finding a needle in a hay stack&#34; because tracing it would do no good. <BR> <BR>If he wanted to throw the police and the media off at anytime-that phone call would have been it. Why use an accent on paper, but not on the phone when people would be able to hear you and possibly identify you? If he was that worried about being caught- he never would have called, he would have let someone else find the victim, but over the phone would have been the ideal time to fake an accent &#40;if that is in fact what he is doing on paper, I for one believe the mistakes are purpose typos, not an accent-my opinion&#41;! He called to report it and did not feel the need to diguise his voice. My opinion only!<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 6745-->
<!--Post: 6759--><!--Time: 1107999313-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST6759"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Lilymarie<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Lilymarie<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, February 09, 2005 - 5:35 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->The only thing for sure is that there are many different opinions about this topic. Some based on facts, some based on instinct. Some more brief and and coherent than others. Some less. <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 6759-->
<!--Post: 6888--><!--Time: 1108063023-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST6888"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:blingbling1169@yahoo.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Cawichitan<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Cawichitan<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, February 10, 2005 - 11:17 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Good observation.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 6888-->
<!--Post: 7019--><!--Time: 1108134372-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST7019"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Ztargate<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Ztargate<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, February 11, 2005 - 7:06 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Lily, I agree with you wholeheartedly. <BR> <BR>But, I remember posting in Yahoo! BBs a few years ago, and I wanting to post as someone else, using new names &#40;don&#39;t ask&#41;. No matter how hard I tried, my friends would find me out by what I said, how I said it, and how I spelled it.  <BR> <BR>Well, one day I decided to fake being a really bad speller, so I wrote my message, then I went back, and I intentionally changed some words to throw my friends off.  <BR> <BR>I fooled my friends for a few weeks, but then someone caught me. I was spelling the long words just fine, while misspelling the short words - something BTK does.  <BR> <BR>The BTK messages remind me of what I did way back then, and I cannot help to think that BTK fakes his illiteracy.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 7019-->
<!--Post: 7141--><!--Time: 1108230943-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST7141"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:blingbling1169@yahoo.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Cawichitan<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Cawichitan<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, February 12, 2005 - 9:55 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Ztargate, I totally understand where you are coming from. A lot of people when leaving ransom notes or other handwriting samples that can be incriminating later try to &#34;disguise&#34; their writing but looping a letter a certain way or changing their normal lingo to fit the charactar they are trying to portray. This is why handwriting experts usually go for the way the letter was written to trace it back to a person, not really looking at what they say although when someone uses one of their favorite catch phrases that helps too!<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 7141-->
